// How to talk to programs on other machines: sockets, UDP and TCP.
//
// An address says where: a machine's IP address and a port on it, a number
// from 1 to 65535 that picks one program on that machine. net_lookup(name,
// port) turns a name like "example.com", or an address written out like
// "192.168.1.20", into a Net_Address. A name with both an IPv4 address and
// an IPv6 one gives the IPv4 one, which reaches more servers. net_text
// writes an address back as text.
//
// UDP sends packets. Each packet arrives whole or not at all, and packets
// can arrive out of order. Nothing is resent, so nothing waits for a lost
// one. That suits a game's moment-to-moment state: when a position is lost,
// a newer one is already on its way. udp_open makes a socket on a port,
// udp_send sends one packet to an address, and udp_receive takes the next
// packet that has arrived, along with its sender's address. Keep packets
// under about 1200 bytes, so they cross any network whole.
//
// TCP is a connection: a stream of bytes each way, arriving all and in
// order. A lost piece is sent again, and what came after it waits. That
// suits what must all arrive: joining a game, chat, a file. One side
// listens on a port (tcp_listen) and takes each connection that comes
// (tcp_accept); the other connects to it (tcp_connect). Then both use
// tcp_send and tcp_receive. TCP doesn't keep the sizes of the sends: two
// sends can arrive as one receive, or one as two. So say how long each
// message is before it.
//
// Nothing here waits for something to arrive. udp_receive, tcp_accept and
// tcp_receive each answer at once, so a game can call them once a frame.
// tcp_send and tcp_connect do wait: tcp_send while the system has no room
// for more, tcp_connect until the other side answers (5 seconds at most,
// unless given another wait).
//
// Where to listen: net_any(port) listens on every address this machine
// has, so other machines can reach it. Windows asks the user, the first
// time, whether to let the program through its firewall. net_local(port)
// listens on 127.0.0.1, which only programs on this machine reach. Port 0
// lets the system pick a free port; the socket's `port` field says which.
//
// This example runs both sides on this machine, one after the other.
module network

use mara.core
use mara.os
use mara.memory

#allocator Arena_Basic(1 * MB)

main :: fun() {
	// UDP: one socket for the server, one for the client.
	server, e := udp_open(net_local(0))
	if e {
		print("couldn't open a socket:", e)
		return
	}
	defer udp_close(&server)
	client, e2 := udp_open(net_local(0))
	if e2 {
		print("couldn't open a socket:", e2)
		return
	}
	defer udp_close(&client)

	hello : [32]byte
	&hello + "hello"
	_ = udp_send(&client, net_local(server.port), hello)

	// Look for a packet each millisecond, as a game would each frame.
	room : [1200!]byte
	window := room[:]
	from := Net_Address()
	for _ in 0:1000 {
		packet, came, re := udp_receive(&server, &window, &from)
		if re {
			print("couldn't receive:", re)
			return
		}
		if came {
			print("the server got % bytes from %", packet.len, net_text(from))
			break
		}
		sleep_ms(1)
	}

	// TCP: a listener, a connection to it, and the connection it accepts.
	listener, le := tcp_listen(net_local(0))
	if le {
		print("couldn't listen:", le)
		return
	}
	defer tcp_listen_stop(&listener)
	conn, ce := tcp_connect(net_local(listener.port))
	if ce {
		print("couldn't connect:", ce)
		return
	}
	defer tcp_close(&conn)
	accepted := Tcp_Connection{id = NO_SOCKET, v4 = false, peer = net_any(0)}
	for _ in 0:1000 {
		took, ae := tcp_accept(&listener, &accepted)
		if ae {
			print("couldn't accept:", ae)
			return
		}
		if took do break
		sleep_ms(1)
	}
	defer tcp_close(&accepted)

	message : [32]byte
	&message + "a message, all of it"
	_ = tcp_send(&conn, message)
	buf : [1200!]byte
	into := buf[:]
	got := 0
	for _ in 0:1000 {
		piece, open, te := tcp_receive(&accepted, &into)
		if te or not open do break
		got += piece.len
		if got == message.len do break
		sleep_ms(1)
	}
	print("the connection carried % bytes", got)
}
